A||||14||BellaFiero||Engine Question - 4 cyl to 4 cyl - what years??||||10||
Z||000000||BellaFiero||09-19-2000||01:14 PM||||I'm buying an engine out of Iowa. Driving up there tomorrow to pick it up from a guy who is going to drop a V8 in his Fiero. He is not sure what year the engine is - pretty sure it is 1986 and will fit in my 88. The previous owner he bought the car from purchased a rebuilt engine and never installed it in the car ( the Fiero itself has a cracked engine - overheated ). So he's got 2 4 cyl engines, one dead and one rebuilt. The plan is to beef this little four cylinder up with serious performance parts and throw it in whenever the 150,000 mile engine in my Bella Donna goes out. ... here's the question - any limitations on my stock automatic coupe? Is there anything i should look out for if I plan to put an '86 four cylinder in my '88 base coupe??||||reg||10
Z||000001||mbramble||09-19-2000||01:41 PM||||Did you recently find this on ebay??  If so, I think the replacement is really an '87 or '88.  He said it was a DIS engine and they were made in 87 and 88.  You can look in Ogre's Cave (link at bottom of the page) to find out the differences in the 4 cylinders, year to year.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||ChadMan||09-19-2000||02:27 PM||||Bella, the 84-86 Duke has a different intake manifold, wiring harness, TBI unit, and ignition system from the 87-88.  If you have the mechanical skills to make the 86 work in your 88, you might as well go ahead with a 2.8 swap!<P>The 84-86 motor has the oil filter on the intake side of the block - the 87-88 has it in the oil pan.  If you see a filter or a distributor on that block, walk away.||||reg||13
Z||000003||DjKid||09-19-2000||02:54 PM||||ChadMan, dun wanna be a correcter or anything, but 87 engines had external oil filters. only the 88's and some 87's had internally balanced stuff. can't remember right off hand what its all called...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Ray - Blue 88 Coupe<BR><A HREF="http://blueknight.carolinafieros.com" TARGET=_blank>My Blue Knight</A> - <A HREF="http://www.carolinafieros.com" TARGET=_blank> Carolina Fieros</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||ChadMan||09-19-2000||03:01 PM||||I believe you, DJ...we all know the General will use up the parts he's got on hand before switching to something new.  So I'm not saying the DIS Duke with external oil filter doesn't exist - I'm just saying I have never seen one that I can recall!<P>If such an animal exists, Bella, I see no reason why it wouldn't work in an 88.  But stick with a DIS Duke or you'll need the wiring harness and ECM from the donor car too.||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||09-19-2000||03:50 PM||||I have a 87 SE 4 banger with a DIS motor and an external oil filter. The balancer 2.5 engines (with the internal oil filter) made their appearance in a few "end of the model year" 87 Fieros and all 88 Fieros (I think). As long as the engine you plan on buying has two coil packs instead of a distributor, it should work in your 88 without changing the ECM or wiring.||||reg||1
Z||000006||DjKid||09-19-2000||05:56 PM||||a 87 engine will work in a 88..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I know cuz thats what my 88 has in it<P>------------------<BR>Ray - Blue 88 Coupe<BR><A HREF="http://blueknight.carolinafieros.com" TARGET=_blank>My Blue Knight</A> - <A HREF="http://www.carolinafieros.com" TARGET=_blank> Carolina Fieros</A>||||reg||1
Z||000007||ur2biteme||09-19-2000||06:40 PM||||i've never seen an '87 WITHOUT an external oil filter.  that's what i'm running.<P>all i know is is that when this engine goes, i want a 6.  a big 6.  gonna go through the pains of changing it out.  dont want another duke myself.||||reg||1
Z||000008||baldlobo||09-19-2000||06:49 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ur2biteme:<BR><B>i've never seen an '87 WITHOUT an external oil filter.  that's what i'm running.<P>all i know is is that when this engine goes, i want a 6.  a big 6.  gonna go through the pains of changing it out.  dont want another duke myself.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>why not go with an SD4 instead of a V-6? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000009||theogre||09-19-2000||11:17 PM||||The DIS motors are both interchangeable. The only dif is wether or not you have the balancer motor. The ECM and the rest of the systems don't care about balancers.<P>Personally I like the Idea of the balancer, but that filter in the pan is B.S.. I'll stay with my non balancer even it it does idle rougher.<P>You don't want to bother with anything older than 87 motor in an 87 or 88 Fiero. <P>The pre87 motors are totally differant. The amount of work to put one in is not worth the effort. It's the same level of work as a 2.8/3.1 V6 install for nowhere near the result.||||reg||1
Z||000010||ur2biteme||09-20-2000||04:09 AM||||heh.  i wouldn't mind the SD4, but with all the money involved.....nevermind.  besides, my gut tells me that i should steer clear of such a small engine putting out that much power for reliability's sake.  not saying that it's not well built, but that it's probably cheaper in the long run to go 3800 because it's a known torque monster and also known for being an ultra reliable engine.  if i'm gonna put a good chunk of change in this puppy, i don't want to have to do it again in a couple years.  just my honest opinion.<P>now that i've gone on that serious streak, i think i just realized that you were giving me a hard time.  oh well.....   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>UR2BITEME<BR>'87 econo coupe<BR>169k and a bad fender :(||||reg||1
Z||000011||baldlobo||09-20-2000||04:47 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ur2biteme:<BR><B>heh.  i wouldn't mind the SD4, but with all the money involved.....nevermind.  besides, my gut tells me that i should steer clear of such a small engine putting out that much power for reliability's sake.  not saying that it's not well built, but that it's probably cheaper in the long run to go 3800 because it's a known torque monster and also known for being an ultra reliable engine.  if i'm gonna put a good chunk of change in this puppy, i don't want to have to do it again in a couple years.  just my honest opinion.<P>now that i've gone on that serious streak, i think i just realized that you were giving me a hard time.  oh well.....    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>A little from row A, a little from row B <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000012||BellaFiero||09-21-2000||09:59 AM||||You guys are the greatest... <BR>So I drive up there ( six hours, aaak! ) and what do I behold but an 88 engine! It's got the internal balancer ( oil filter inside the plug ) and the original owner couldn't use it for his '84 Fiero. When the guy I bought it from purchased the car and engine, he planned to throw a V8 in it. SO I got an 88 engine for $71.00 <BR>Not bad, if I do say so myself. But gas was $40!! In a Ford Aerostar for twelve hours yesterday. ( But don't knock the Aerostars... they come in handy and get pretty good mileage for what you can throw in the back... engines, parts, panels... kids... )  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000013||ChadMan||09-21-2000||01:18 PM||||$71!?!  That's a fantastic deal.  Didn't you say it was rebuilt?  That's even better...<P>What do you plan to do to hop this motor up?  I know you can hog out the intake and exhaust ports, and I know Accel makes hipo coils for the DIS motor, but what else can you do with a DIS Duke?<P>I'm asking in earnest, not being sarcastic - I'm really curious.  Plus I'm still humbled from being wrong on the 87/external filter thing.||||reg||14
Z||000014||BellaFiero||09-21-2000||04:10 PM||||The engine was supposedly rebuilt - but that's of little consequence. Ideally, this is the replacement engine for the one in my car now. She runs fine, but you have to be prepared for the little things in life later on. So I'll have me an engine in the garage to toss in ( making it sound like you can do it in an hour instead of three days, right?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ) I'm going to dismantle this engine, get her rebored, new gaskets and pistons, etc. Do the whole rebuild kit. Have it sandblasted and cleaned, probably throw some hi-temp red paint on her. Get the crank done downtown by a machine shop, the head rebuilt, etc. Probably a new fly wheel too. Ideally, what I would like to do is give her a little more power. I'm wary of the coils because I've heard bad things about combining Accell and DIS engines. <BR>I've got to figure out what's more important to me. I can hop her up a bit in the low end, give her a bit more growl at the stop lights, but ultimately I'll lose from the top end... highway mileage and power is going to bite. <P><P>------------------<BR>"No, no... you can't put that there, it'll be in the way of the Johnson Rod!"  ||||reg||10
